    "summary": "Acoustic debut of Box of Rain plus a soaring Dark Star",
    "blurb": "The show opens with an all‑acoustic set that delivers the first ever performance of \"Box of Rain\" and a beautifully rendered acoustic medley. A marathon second set features a monumental \"Dark Star\" that stretches into the early morning. Audio is muddy throughout, but the historic playing shines through.",
    "review": "On September 17, 1970 at the Fillmore East the Dead offered a rare acoustic‑focused first set that would become legendary: Jerry Garcia slipped onto piano and pedal steel for an intimate rendition of \"Box of Rain,\" while Phil Lesh supplied resonant vocals and solid bass. Bob Weir’s harmonies and the New Riders of the Purple Sage guests added extra texture, making the acoustic medley (including \"Brokedown Palace\" and \"Ripple\") a standout moment. After a brief intermission the band launched into an electric marathon, highlighted by a soaring \"Dark Star\" that segued into two rounds of \"Good Lovin'\" with dual drum passages. Though every tape of this night is audience‑sourced and suffers from muffling, hiss, and occasional drop‑outs, the performance’s historical importance—especially the debut of \"Box of Rain\"—makes it a must‑listen for completists despite the sonic shortcomings.",
